  "worked_solution": "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222139/assets/e1774466f020515d3296f2e48fda0a61154bddbfd2285a67c97bdc7e35b60da5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(2{x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
